+---
+tier: spec
+created: 2026-09-20
+status: clarifying
+owner: node
+branch: HEAD
+touches: [src/flask/ctx.py, tests/test_appctx.py, tests/test_basic.py]
+---
+
+# d17-teardown-errors
+
+## Why
+An exception from a teardown callback currently prevents later teardown
+callbacks and the teardown signal from running. It can also prevent the full
+request/application cleanup sequence from reporting every teardown failure.
+
+## What
+ - Outcome: request and application-context teardown attempt every callback and
+   their teardown signal, then report collected teardown errors after cleanup.
+ - Outcome: context-local state is restored before a teardown error reaches the caller.
+ - Non-goal: change registration order, callback arguments, or non-teardown
+   signal dispatch behavior.
+
+## How
+Use one internal collector for both teardown paths. It calls each cleanup action
+independently, stores failures, and raises a `BaseExceptionGroup` on Python
+3.11+ (or the first error on Python 3.10). `AppContext.pop` passes one collector
+through request and application teardown so final context restoration precedes
+the aggregated raise.
+
+## Alternatives
+- Let each teardown path raise independently: rejected because a request
+  teardown error can otherwise be replaced by application teardown failure and
+  context cleanup cannot retain every error.
+
+## Impact
+- `AppContext.pop` is the sole combined request/application cleanup lifetime;
+  it resets `_cv_app` and sends `appcontext_popped` in its `finally` block.
+- `Flask.do_teardown_request` is reached for request contexts and invokes
+  blueprint then app callbacks; `do_teardown_appcontext` is also used by manual
+  app contexts and CLI contexts.
+- Teardown registration lives in the sans-I/O scaffold; callback order and
+  callback `exc` arguments remain compatible.
+
+## Acceptance
+- [x] Both teardown lifetimes attempt later callbacks and their signal after
+  earlier callbacks fail. — test: `tests/test_appctx.py`
+- [x] Python 3.11+ exposes both distinct failures as one exception group; older
+  Python exposes the first failure. — test: `tests/test_appctx.py`
+- [x] A request teardown failure still permits application teardown and leaves
+  no active application context before it is raised. — test: `tests/test_appctx.py`
+
+## Open questions
+None.
+
+## Decisions
+- context-teardown: error collection is shared between request and application
+  teardown so the combined context lifetime preserves all teardown failures.
